## Overview

`httpapi-client` provides the shared foundation for CLI, AI toolkit, and MCP adapters. `HttpApiSpec.operations` contains each selected operation's name, reflected Effect `inputSchema`, derived `inputJsonSchema`, and read-only, destructive, and idempotent annotations. OpenAPI supplies operation metadata; validation comes from the endpoint's Effect schemas rather than reconstructed OpenAPI schemas.

Inputs use `{ params, query, headers, body }`. Path, query, and header values use decoded endpoint types: pass a number such as `{ query: { days: 7 } }`, not its wire string. Payload codecs retain their transformations, such as decoding an ISO string into a Date. The generated `HttpApiClient` handles transport encoding and response decoding.

## Installation And Imports

Install the runtime package with `bun add @krak-stack/registry` and use the imports below. Alternatively, install the `@krak-stack/httpapi-client` registry item with shadcn and replace the package imports with `@/lib/httpapi-client` and `@/lib/httpapi-helpers`. Registry item names are installation identifiers, not npm module paths.

## Usage

```ts
import { Layer } from "effect";
import { FetchHttpClient } from "effect/http";
import { ApiClient } from "@krak-stack/registry/httpapi/client";
import { HttpApiSpec } from "@krak-stack/registry/httpapi/helpers";
import { AppApi } from "@/api";

export const apiClientLayer = ApiClient.layer({
  api: AppApi,
  baseUrl: "https://example.com",
}).pipe(Layer.provide(FetchHttpClient.layer));

export const apiSpecLayer = HttpApiSpec.layer({
  api: AppApi,
  methods: ["get", "post"],
});
```

`executeHttpApiOperation({ operation, input }, client)` supplies empty parameter, query, and header maps when omitted and delegates to `ApiClient.execute`. Validate untrusted input with the operation's `inputSchema` before executing; the toolkit and CLI do this at their boundaries. Keep API configuration, authenticated HTTP clients, and application handlers in the consuming application.
